SAFETY='''<h2>Safety basics (every cook)</h2><ul><li><b>Outdoors only.</b> Never use a charcoal grill indoors, in a garage, tent, camper, boat cabin or vehicle. Burning charcoal makes carbon monoxide, which you cannot see or smell.</li><li><b>Stable, level, non-combustible surface.</b> Bare soil, gravel, sand, concrete, pavers or a fire-safe pad. The Compact's 2⅞" air gap between the table and the bottom of the coal bed helps minimize radiant heat to the surface below, but it is still a live fire.</li><li><b>Clearance.</b> Keep it away from tents, vehicles, siding, overhangs, dry grass and anything that can burn. Keep kids and pets back.</li><li><b>Follow local rules.</b> Burn bans, campground, park, venue and HOA rules come first.</li><li><b>Water or a fire extinguisher nearby</b>, plus heat-safe gloves for handling grates and panels.</li><li><b>Ash stays hot for a long time.</b> Put it in a metal container with a lid and never pack hot steel or ash into a vehicle.</li></ul>'''
TEMPS='''<h2>Safe internal temperatures</h2><p>Guessing is how small-grill cooks go wrong, because everything happens fast over a close fire. Use an <a href="'''+THERMO+'''">instant-read thermometer</a> in the thickest part, away from bone. USDA minimum internal temperatures:</p><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Food</th><th>Minimum internal temp</th></tr><tr><td>Beef, pork, veal and lamb steaks, chops and roasts</td><td>145°F, then rest 3 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Ground beef, pork, veal and lamb</td><td>160°F</td></tr><tr><td>Poultry (whole, parts, ground)</td><td>165°F</td></tr><tr><td>Fish and shellfish</td><td>145°F, or flesh opaque and separates easily with a fork</td></tr><tr><td>Egg dishes</td><td>160°F; cook eggs until yolk and white are firm</td></tr><tr><td>Leftovers and casseroles</td><td>165°F</td></tr></table></div>'''
FIRE='''<h2>Fire setup on the Compact</h2><p>The Compact's cooking area is 16.25" × 9" (146 sq in). That is plenty for one to three people, but it means coal placement does most of the work a dial does on other cookers. Use a <a href="/two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill.html">two-zone fire</a>: lit coals banked across about two-thirds of the length, the last third thin or empty. The hot side sears; the cool side finishes, holds and rescues food from flare-ups. Light with a chimney or one of the <a href="/how-to-light-charcoal-without-lighter-fluid.html">no-lighter-fluid methods</a>.</p>'''
GEAR='''<ul><li><a href="'''+COMPACT+'''">Slot Grill Compact</a>, seasoned (<a href="/how-to-season-a-compact-grill.html">how</a>)</li><li>Chimney starter, charcoal or cooking wood, newspaper or natural fire starters, a lighter</li><li>Long tongs and a sturdy spatula</li><li>Heat-safe gloves</li><li><a href="'''+THERMO+'''">Instant-read thermometer</a></li><li>Metal can with a lid for cold ash</li><li>Sheet pan or foil pan for raw-to-grill and a clean one for cooked food</li></ul>'''
def recipe2(intro,serves,time,ingr,steps,why="",variations=None,trouble=None,serve="",fire=True,extra=""):
    h=intro+f'<p><b>Serves:</b> {serves} · <b>Total time:</b> {time} · <b>Grill:</b> <a href="{COMPACT}">Slot Grill Compact</a></p>'
    if why: h+='<h2>Why this works on the Compact</h2>'+why
    h+='<h2>Ingredients</h2><ul>'+"".join(f"<li>{i}</li>" for i in ingr)+'</ul><h2>Gear</h2>'+GEAR
    if fire: h+=FIRE
    h+='<h2>Step by step</h2><ol>'+"".join(f"<li>{s}</li>" for s in steps)+'</ol>'+extra
    if variations: h+='<h2>Variations</h2><ul>'+"".join(f"<li>{v}</li>" for v in variations)+'</ul>'
    if trouble: h+='<h2>Troubleshooting</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix</th></tr>'+"".join(f"<tr><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td></tr>" for a,b in trouble)+'</table></div>'
    if serve: h+='<h2>Serving, leftovers and cleanup</h2>'+serve
    return h+TEMPS
MISTAKES='''<h2>Common small-grill mistakes (and the fix)</h2><ol><li><b>Cooking before the coals are ready.</b> Black-topped coals give smoky, weak heat and sticking. Wait until they glow under a light coat of gray ash.</li><li><b>Wall-to-wall coals.</b> With no cool zone, every flare-up becomes a burn. Leave at least a third of the firebox thin or empty.</li><li><b>Crowding the grate.</b> 146 sq in fills fast. Leave space between pieces so you can move them, and cook in waves.</li><li><b>Moving food too early.</b> Let it sear and release on its own; it sticks until a crust forms.</li><li><b>Cold, dirty grates.</b> Preheat for a few minutes, scrape, and wipe with an oiled paper towel held in tongs.</li><li><b>Wet food.</b> Pat meat and vegetables dry before oiling and seasoning. Moisture steams instead of searing.</li><li><b>Judging doneness by time or color.</b> Small fires vary. Use a thermometer.</li><li><b>Leaving ash in the firebox.</b> Old ash chokes airflow. Empty it once cold before the next cook.</li><li><b>Putting the grill away wet.</b> Raw carbon steel needs to be dried and lightly oiled. See <a href="/rust-prevention-carbon-steel-grill.html">rust prevention</a>.</li></ol>'''
PACKDOWN='''<h2>After the cook: burn-down and pack-down</h2><ol><li>Let the remaining coals burn down. Don't leave a fire unattended.</li><li>When ash and steel are completely cold, dump the ash into a metal container with a lid, or dispose of it where the venue or campground allows.</li><li>Scrape the grates, then wipe the cooking surfaces with a light coat of oil or <a href="'''+BALM+'''">Slot Grill Seasoning Balm</a>.</li><li>Lift off the grates, slide out the V side panels, pull the end pieces and stack. The Compact packs to about 1" thick.</li><li>Store it dry. A bag or old box keeps soot off the rest of your gear.</li></ol>'''
QUICK='''<h2>Quick-start checklist</h2><ul><li>Grill seasoned and dry; old ash emptied</li><li>Stable, level, non-combustible spot with clearance</li><li>Charcoal lit in a chimney, ashed over</li><li>Two-zone fire built, grates preheated and oiled</li><li>Thermometer, tongs, spatula and gloves within reach</li><li>Clean tray ready for cooked food</li></ul>'''
ONROAD='''<h2>Taking this recipe on the road</h2><p>The Compact packs about 1" thick and weighs about 14 lb, so this recipe travels to a campsite, tailgate, river bank or job site as easily as it cooks on the patio. A few habits make it safer and easier away from a kitchen:</p><ul><li><b>Prep at home.</b> Trim, season or marinate meat, chop vegetables and mix sauces in the kitchen. Pack each in a sealed bag or container labeled by cook order.</li><li><b>Keep raw meat cold and separate.</b> Pack it at the bottom of the cooler, sealed, below ready-to-eat food. Keep the cooler at 40°F or below with plenty of ice, and keep it in the shade.</li><li><b>Two sets of tongs and platters:</b> one for raw, one for cooked. Never put cooked food back on the tray that held raw meat.</li><li><b>Bring water and soap</b> for hands and tools, plus paper towels and a trash bag.</li><li><b>Pack the thermometer.</b> It matters more outdoors, where wind and cold change cook times.</li><li><b>Plan for ash.</b> Bring a metal can with a lid and follow the venue's disposal rules.</li></ul><p>For more, see the <a href="/compact-grill-camping-guide.html">camping</a>, <a href="/compact-grill-tailgate-guide.html">tailgate</a> and <a href="/fishing-and-river-trip-grilling.html">river trip</a> guides.</p>'''
_r2=recipe2
def recipe2(*a,**k): return _r2(*a,**k)+ONROAD
WEATHER='''<h2>Cooking in wind, rain, cold and heat</h2><div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Conditions</th><th>What happens</th><th>What to do</th></tr><tr><td>Wind</td><td>Coals burn hotter and faster on the windward side; ash and sparks blow</td><td>Shelter the grill behind a vehicle, wall or rocks with safe clearance; point the hot end into the wind; skip cooking in high wind or red-flag conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Rain or drizzle</td><td>Slower lighting, weaker coals, wet fuel</td><td>Keep charcoal in a dry bag or lidded bin; cook in the open (never under a tent, awning or in a garage); dry and oil the grill afterward</td></tr><tr><td>Cold</td><td>Steel and food pull heat; longer cooks</td><td>Light more fuel; keep coals together; add lit coals sooner; use the thermometer, not the clock</td></tr><tr><td>Heat</td><td>Food safety window shrinks; you're sweating over a fire</td><td>Keep coolers shaded and cold; don't leave food out more than 1 hour above 90°F; stay hydrated</td></tr><tr><td>Dry season</td><td>Sparks can start fires</td><td>Check burn bans; clear debris around the grill; keep water ready</td></tr></table></div>'''
